A final-year MSc course on epigenetics: the biology of chromatin and DNA methylation, the sequencing assays that measure them, and how to analyse the data. Draft outcomes, to be aligned with the syllabus once it exists.


By the end of the course, a student should be able to take an epigenomic dataset (ATAC-seq, ChIP-seq or CUT&Tag, bisulfite sequencing or Hi-C), process it from reads to biological interpretation, and judge whether the experiment and the analysis support the claims made.



A student can:

explain how chromatin structure, histone modifications, DNA methylation and 3D genome organisation regulate gene expression;
describe the principle of ATAC-seq, ChIP-seq (and CUT&RUN/CUT&Tag), bisulfite sequencing and Hi-C, and the biases each assay introduces;
explain the statistical models behind peak calling, differential accessibility/binding, differential methylation and Hi-C normalisation.




A student can:

process raw reads from an epigenomic assay to aligned, filtered reads and signal tracks on the command line, and assess their quality;
call peaks, methylation levels and 3D genome features, and test for differences between conditions in R;
work with genomic intervals and annotations (bedtools, GenomicRanges), and visualise data in a genome browser;
integrate epigenomic data with gene expression to propose regulatory mechanisms.




An individual written exam. Exercises with code build understanding; the exam tests reading data figures, small calculations and judging designs and claims.



A student can:

critically assess the design, controls, normalisation and interpretation of published epigenomic studies, including claims of epigenetic inheritance.

